SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 306 | Friday, March 6, 2026

Good morning. Friday, so a quick one.

1) Transportation and warehousing added 4,000 jobs in February
Fifteenth flat month in a row, essentially. Average hourly earnings in warehousing rose 4.1% year on year, which is above the economy-wide number and the reason every automation vendor's sales deck opens with a wage chart.

2) Force majeure on a Gulf Coast ethylene cracker
A compressor failure took a major Texas cracker offline for what the operator says is "four to six weeks." Polyethylene spot prices rose 8 cents a pound in two days. Film and packaging converters are the first to feel it; the rest of you will feel it in your packaging invoices in May.

3) Port of Oakland extends terminal gate hours
The largest terminal at Oakland will run a Saturday gate from March 21 through the summer peak and add a 6pm to 10pm weekday window at reduced fees. Local truckers have asked for this for years. The port's stated goal is to pull some Central Valley ag exports back from LA/LB, where they have drifted since 2021.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 9. Blank sailings announced for the second half of March on the transpacific, down from eleven in the first half. Carriers think demand is coming back. We will see.

Have a good weekend.

